From 138fe3739cbba14f71b89e1b9f3776b339f0fc6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 14:54:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 077/105] input: let a pass end where its data ends, not a pcr
 step past the last pcr

The resume point for a repeat is taken from how far the data the demuxer has
handed over reaches, bounded below by the last pcr and above by one pcr
cadence step past it. The upper bound was there for demuxers whose dates run
ahead of their pcr, ts and mpeg-ps being the two named, so that a pass was
left where it was rather than pushed out past a hole.

Both of those have since been held back where it belongs, in the demuxer, and
the bound now catches only containers that carry their timestamps in cluster
or packet headers. Their pcr is emitted before the blocks it covers have all
been handed over, so an end further out than a cadence step past it is
ordinary: matroska ends 48 ms past a pcr it steps 23 ms at a time, and the
bound cut every pass 25 ms short of its own content. Nothing plays in that
25 ms - the next pass simply begins there - so it is not a hole but a piece of
the item that is never heard, and the drift it leaves accumulates until the
output's correction saturates and is heard as detuning.

Measured on a null sink, six repeats of a 4 s item, counting the passes where
the drift correction sat at its 9-cent limit:

    matroska    4 saturations  ->  0, pass 3986 ms -> 4011 ms (its length)
    ts          3              ->  3   unchanged, 375 ms inside the bound
    mpeg-ps     0              ->  0   unchanged, 200 ms inside
    flac        3              ->  2   unchanged, exactly on it
    wav         0              ->  0   unchanged
    ogg         0              ->  0   never reaches this path

Only matroska was against the bound. The ts saturations are a separate matter
and are not touched here.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/input/es_out.c | 17 ++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/input/es_out.c b/src/input/es_out.c
index 646387490d..b7336f5bd9 100644
--- a/src/input/es_out.c
+++ b/src/input/es_out.c
@@ -735,14 +735,21 @@ static void EsOutRepeatShift( es_out_t *out, es_out_pgrm_t *p_pgrm,
      * output playing silence across the difference.
      *
      * The data the demuxer has already handed over says where the pass really
-     * ended, so take that when it is nearer. Never take it further out than
-     * the cadence: a demuxer whose dates run ahead of its pcr would otherwise
-     * push the next pass past a hole rather than close one, and never nearer
-     * than the pcr itself, which is ground already covered. */
+     * ended, so take that, and the cadence only when there is none to take.
+     * Never nearer than the pcr itself, which is ground already covered.
+     *
+     * It is not bounded above by the cadence. A container that carries its
+     * timestamps in cluster or packet headers emits the pcr before the blocks
+     * it covers have all been handed over, so an end further out than one
+     * cadence step past it is ordinary rather than the mark of a demuxer
+     * running ahead: matroska ends 48 ms past a pcr it steps 23 ms at a time,
+     * and the bound cost it 25 ms of its own content at every loop. A demuxer
+     * whose pcr genuinely outruns its data is held back where that belongs,
+     * in the demuxer. */
     const vlc_tick_t i_cadence = p_pgrm->i_last_pcr + p_pgrm->i_pcr_step;
     vlc_tick_t i_resume = p_pgrm->i_last_end;
 
-    if( i_resume <= VLC_TICK_INVALID || i_resume > i_cadence )
+    if( i_resume <= VLC_TICK_INVALID )
         i_resume = i_cadence;
     if( i_resume < p_pgrm->i_last_pcr )
         i_resume = p_pgrm->i_last_pcr;
